{
  "dom_id": "step-t15549",
  "name": "Calculating the Length of a Part of a Circle",
  "problem": "The problem of finding the length of a circle or a part of a circle often appears in real life. \n\nFor example, consider the pie in the shape of a circle divided into six equal slices, as shown in the figure below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14183030/assets/48b416b9dffbf6e3e746e3fa8f90ec8043f937fe612e36e1219b5e9e8c368f22.png)\n\n How to find the total length of the border of one slice of pie?\n\nThe border of the slice of pie will consist of two parts:\n\n- the top and the bottom parts, which consist of two radii;\n\n- the right part (circular part), which consists of one-sixth of the circumference.\n\nFirst, let's find the length of the circular part. The formula for the circumference of a circle is ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14183030/assets/636374af24eb4d5c435de97b8e1ac460148deba4916b0954e3d1d2f7a364f9d7.svg) where ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14183030/assets/3122e2ea79ab0512edfce5c875a099de1a7067581b658d1b8fc9c105277aed36.svg) is the radius. Therefore, we have \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14183030/assets/15bff81cb45d3df680e782e69534d2489388dfbcfd12b3cd579298d03f8bd452.svg)\n\nFinally, the length of the border is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14183030/assets/15037adbdea6b21d54991917b5dad6108c60257fd8f509300642343a101ebeeb.svg)",
  "worked_solution": "",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
  "html": "<div id=\"step-t15549\" class=\"step\" style=\"display: block;\">\n    \n               \n                <div class=\"tutorial\">\n                    <div class=\"stepHeader\">\n                        <div class=\"stepName\"><span class=\"stepType\">Calculating the Length of a Part of a Circle</span></div>       \n                        <div class=\"helpButton\" stepid=\"t15549\">?</div>\n                    </div>                    \n    \n                    <p>The problem of finding the length of a circle or a part of a circle often appears in real life. </p>\n<p>For example, consider the pie in the shape of a circle divided into six equal slices, as shown in the figure below.</p>\n<div id=\"graphic-2e009104679f2c85b3cc41d47a17431c\" class=\"graphic\"><img src=\"/graphics/2e009104679f2c85b3cc41d47a17431c\"></div>\n\n<p> How to find the total length of the border of one slice of pie?</p>\n<p>The border of the slice of pie will consist of two parts:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>the top and the bottom parts, which consist of two radii;</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>the right part (circular part), which consists of one-sixth of the circumference.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>First, let's find the length of the circular part. The formula for the circumference of a circle is <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.439ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"8.807ex\" height=\"2.034ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -705 3892.6 899\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><path data-c=\"1D436\" d=\"M50 252Q50 367 117 473T286 641T490 704Q580 704 633 653Q642 643 648 636T656 626L657 623Q660 623 684 649Q691 655 699 663T715 679T725 690L740 705H746Q760 705 760 698Q760 694 728 561Q692 422 692 421Q690 416 687 415T669 413H653Q647 419 647 422Q647 423 648 429T650 449T651 481Q651 552 619 605T510 659Q484 659 454 652T382 628T299 572